SECTION B — RUNTIME DEPENDENCIES OF THE DISTRIBUTED CORE
================================================================================

Installed alongside the `neuroedge` package. Versions pinned in
python/requirements-lock.txt. All permissive.

| Package                  | Licence        | Verified   | Used for                          |
|--------------------------|----------------|------------|-----------------------------------|
| typer                    | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | CLI framework                     |
| rich                     | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | Terminal rendering                |
| pydantic                 | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | Typed models                      |
| jsonschema               | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | Schema validation (draft 2020-12) |
| pyyaml                   | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | Gate document parsing             |
| rfc8785                  | Apache-2.0     | 2026-09-21 | RFC 8785 canonical JSON (JCS)     |
| deepdiff                 | MIT            | 2026-09-21 | Golden-reference diffing          |

Transitive dependencies and their licences are recorded in full by the
`licences` artifact of every `ci-sim-linux` run.

Two deliberate choices in this section, both to keep GPL out of the core:

  * jsonschema is declared as `jsonschema[format-nongpl]`, NOT `jsonschema[format]`.
    The default `format` extra pulls in `rfc3987`, which is GPL-licensed. The
    `format-nongpl` extra uses `rfc3987-syntax` (MIT) instead. The extra is
    required, not cosmetic: without a format checker, `format: date-time` and
    `format: uri` in trace.v1.json are annotations only, and a trace with an
    unparseable timestamp would validate.

  * copier is NOT a dependency. It depends on `jinja2-ansible-filters`, which
    is GPL3, so `neuroedge new` (TSK-S3-07) uses a plain-Python generator
    (python/neuroedge/templates/) instead. Do not add copier, or any template
    engine with a copyleft dependency, without a written licence decision.

  * gpiod (libgpiod v2 Python bindings) is NOT a core dependency. It is
    LGPL-2.1-or-later and sits in the optional `linux` extra, needed only by
    target `linux` (python/neuroedge/hal/linux.py, TSK-S3-05). It is imported
    at run time and never vendored or statically linked, so the MIT core
    carries no LGPL code; installing the extra brings LGPL code into that
    environment only. The CI licence gate installs `dev` alone and still
    rejects any GPL-family licence in the core set.

  * mcp (the official Model Context Protocol Python SDK, MIT) is NOT a core
    dependency. It sits in the optional `mcp` extra, needed only by
    `neuroedge mcp serve` (python/neuroedge/mcp_server.py, Q-24). Every
    transitive dependency is MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0 or PSF (checked 2026-09-23).
    It is also in `dev`, so the MCP server is tested in CI and the licence
    gate sees its dependency set.

  * litellm (BerriAI, MIT) is NOT a core dependency. It sits in the optional
    `cloud` extra, pinned at litellm==1.102.0 (Q-10, Q-11), and is imported
    only by python/neuroedge/models/providers/ when an agent declares
    [system_two] (TSK-S2-11). Used as a library, never its proxy server; the
    wheel has no `enterprise/` directory. Its transitive set is held to the
    Q-11 policy — MIT, BSD-2/3-Clause, Apache-2.0, ISC, PSF, MPL-2.0 used
    unmodified — by the CI job `cloud-extra` (scripts/check_licences.py);
    GPL/LGPL/AGPL, SSPL, BSL, commercial and unknown licences fail it.
    Checked 2026-09-24: MPL-2.0 packages are certifi, fqdn and tqdm
    (tqdm: MPL-2.0 AND MIT); regex is Apache-2.0 AND CNRI-Python. Not in
    `dev` (~120 MB): the tests use a fake `litellm` module.


